course: chinese-street
lesson: 9
title: Family: mum, dad, brothers and sisters
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: In English a brother is just a brother. In Mandarin he is always older (gēge) or younger (dìdi), and a sister is jiějie (older) or mèimei (younger). Who is older matters.
explain: Ming's little sister never calls him «Ming»; she calls him gēge, like a title. Any big girl in the playground is jiějie to the little ones.
explain: «māma!» across the flat means mum — keep it high, or it sounds like mà (scold). bàba is dad, and older kids joke about lǎobà (old dad), but only about their own.
explain: When Ming says «lái wǒ jiā wánr ba» (come and play at my house), jiā is both home and family, and the invitation is real.
fact: māma («MAH-ma», the first high, the second light) is mum; with a falling first tone it sounds like mà, to scold.
fact: bàba («BAH-ba», the first falling) is dad; bā, high and level, is eight.
fact: gēge («GUH-guh») is big brother, also an older boy a child looks up to; when you talk about one brother, you nearly always say whether he is older or younger.
fact: jiějie («JYEH-jyeh») is big sister and any big girl to little children; younger ones never call her by her name.
fact: dìdi («DEE-dee») is little brother, said with love and also in «dìdi, bié nào!» (little brother, stop being annoying!).
fact: mèimei («MAY-may») is little sister and a sweet name for any little girl.
fact: wǒ jiā («waw JYAH») means my home and my family; «dào wǒ jiā» is a real invitation.
fact: nǐ yǒu xiōngdì jiěmèi ma? means have you got brothers or sisters?; answers are yǒu, wǒ yǒu yí ge dìdi, or méiyǒu.
fact: dúshēngzǐ («DOO?-shung-dz») is an only child (for a girl dúshēngnǚ); it is not sad or strange, and many parents who grew up in China are only children themselves.
fact: bǎobǎo («BAOW-baow») means baby or sweetie; a friend may go red if his mum calls him that in front of you, so don't tease him with it.
fact: wǒ mā («waw MAH») is a casual «my mum» among kids, as in «wǒ mā shuō bù xíng» (my mum says no); his mum is āyí to you.
fact: lǎobà («LAOW-bah») is a fond «old dad» that older kids use about their own dad; it is not for someone else's.
exercise: What is māma? | Mum | Horse | Granny | Big sister
exercise: How do you say dad? | bàba | bā | lǎoshī | gēge
exercise: Why must mā in māma stay high? | With a fall it sounds like mà, to scold | With a fall it turns into the word for dad | Low means grandmother | It does not matter at all, any tone works
exercise: What is gēge? | Big brother | Little brother | Any brother | Uncle
exercise: Who is jiějie to a little child? | A big sister, or any big girl | Only a teacher at the school gate | A baby girl in the family | A granny
exercise: dìdi is | Little brother | Big brother | Dad | Cousin
exercise: «dìdi, bié nào!» means | Little brother, stop being annoying! | Little brother, come over here now! | Little brother, well done! | Little brother, good night, sleep well!
exercise: What is mèimei? | Little sister | Big sister | Mum | A kind of fish
exercise: Ming talks about his one brother. What will you hear? | gēge or dìdi, depending on who is older | gēge, for any brother at all, older or younger | mèimei, for any brother | bàba, for any brother
exercise: What does jiā mean in wǒ jiā? | Home and family | School | Friend | Food
exercise: Ming says «lái wǒ jiā wánr ba». What is it? | A real invitation to play at his house | A joke he doesn't really mean | A warning to keep away from his house | A goodbye
exercise: How do you ask if someone has brothers or sisters? | nǐ yǒu xiōngdì jiěmèi ma? | nǐ jiào shénme míngzi? | nǐ jǐ suì le? | nǐ qù nǎr?
exercise: What is a dúshēngzǐ? | An only child | A twin | A baby | A big brother
exercise: Ming's mum calls him bǎobǎo. What does it mean? | Baby, sweetie | Big boy | Naughty one | Bread roll
exercise: Ming says «wǒ mā shuō bù xíng». What did his mum say? | No | Yes | Come home | Hello
exercise: What do you call Ming's mum? | āyí | wǒ mā | lǎobà | jiějie
exercise: Who is lǎobà for? | Older kids about their own dad | Any old man you meet in the street | Ming's dad, said by you | A teacher
test: «ming's sister calls him gēge not Ming, weird» Why? | Younger ones call an older brother gēge, like a title | She forgot his name, she's only little | gēge means «silly», she's teasing him lol, it's banter | It's a nickname for Ming only
test: You call Ming's mum «mā» at dinner. What would happen? | It sounds odd: she's your āyí, not your mum | She would feel honoured that you're family now | It means auntie | It's the right word for guests to use
test: «haha ming's mum called him bǎobǎo, let's all call him baby» What is kind? | Don't tease him with it | Call him bǎobǎo all week | Tell the whole class | Make a song about it
test: Ming has no brothers or sisters. What is true? | He is a dúshēngzǐ, and that's not sad or strange | Something must be wrong with his family, surely | He must be very lonely all the time | Only children are not allowed in China
test: «ask his dad, lǎobà, can we go park» What is wrong? | lǎobà is for your own dad; say shūshu | Nothing, it's polite for any friend's dad | lǎobà means grandad | lǎobà is what kids call teachers
test: What would happen if you shouted «mà!» with a falling tone across the flat? | It would sound like «scold», not «mum» | Mum would come running at once | It means «dad», so dad would come running | It means «eight»
test: Your friend's big cousin is 15, a girl. What does Ming's little sister call her? | jiějie | mèimei | āyí | dìdi
test: In a cartoon a dragon calls his older brother «dìdi». What is wrong? | dìdi is little brother; older is gēge | Nothing, dìdi works for any brother | dìdi is a girl's name | Dragons have no brothers in Chinese stories
test: You say «wǒ yǒu yí ge dìdi». What did you tell Ming? | I have a little brother | I have a big brother | I'm a little brother | I have no brothers
test: «nǐ yǒu xiōngdì jiěmèi ma?» — you're an only child. What do you say? | méiyǒu | shì | duì | bú yào
test: «bā!» you shout, high and level, meaning dad. What did Ming hear? | Eight | Dad | Mum | Hello
exam: Identify the term by which a child customarily addresses an elder brother. | gēge | dìdi | mèimei | bàba
exam: Ascertain the principal distinction drawn in Mandarin terms for siblings. | Siblings are designated by whether they are older or younger | Siblings are designated solely by their given names, as in English | No distinction of sex is drawn in any sibling term | Siblings are designated according to height
exam: Select the response consistent with the established meaning of the word jiā. | It denotes both a home and a family | It denotes a school or place of study | It denotes a neighbour | It denotes a holiday spent away
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the term dúshēngzǐ? | It denotes an only child, with no connotation of misfortune | It denotes a twin | It is a term of reproach directed at spoilt children | It denotes the eldest of numerous siblings
